--- body ---
## Context
Child of 1074. Without `EnterWorktree` the session cwd stays in the primary
checkout, so Edit/Write land on main; the guard's 3 clear catches were of
that kind. Removing the opt-in needs a replacement for "who moves the session".

## Scope
1. Experiment once 1076 lands: from a session not entered in a worktree, spawn
   a subagent without the isolation parameter and run `git worktree add`;
   also from a background job. Record whether the runtime guard still fires.
   Also record `echo $PPID` from a session Bash call, end the session, and
   `ps -p` it: the 1076 lock reason assumes that pid lives exactly as long as
   the session (Fable seat on PR #1313).
2. Report to the author with the result; the author decides removal of the
   opt-in in `scripts/on-start.sh` and `rules/claude-code.md`; if removed, the
   `ExitWorktree` steps in skills/lair (step 26) and skills/roar (step 9b-c)
   and skills/raid:358 go with it (roar sweep, PR #1313).

## Experiment (padme, 2026-10-14, Claude Code, Opus 5.5 session, Haiku probes)
Probe: `worktree add --detach` + `remove` of a scratch tree, against the
primary checkout.

| Session state | Caller | `git -C <primary>` | plain `git` |
|---|---|---|---|
| not entered | session Bash | ok | ok |
| not entered | background Bash job | — | ok |
| not entered | subagent, no isolation | ok | — |
| entered (`EnterWorktree path=`) | session Bash | **refused** (positive control) | ok, run from the worktree |
| entered | subagent, no isolation | **refused**, add and remove | ok: add, lock, unlock, remove |
| entered | session Bash, toward a sibling worktree | **refused**, `-C` and `cd` alike | — |

- The guard fires only after the session enters a worktree, and a
  non-isolated subagent inherits it from its parent. Unentered, nothing
  fired, background job included.
- Refusal text: "redirects git to the shared checkout via -C". The same
  `worktree add` without `-C`, run from inside the entered worktree, passes:
  the git common dir is shared. hunt step 3 (`git -C "$R" worktree add`)
  is therefore refused in an entered session as written.
- `$PPID`: a subagent's Bash reports the session's own pid. The pid is
  the runtime process, not the session: this conversation's pid had lived
  2h45 since an earlier session's launch, through `/clear`. A headless
  `claude -p` pid was gone once it exited. 1076's lock therefore outlives
  the session in interactive use (safe direction: kept locked, never
  unlocked early) and matches the session in headless use.
- The guard refuses git toward any tree other than the session's own,
  not only the primary: `/gaze` seats reading `git -C "$review_tree"` stay
  blocked whatever the recipe. Dropping `-C` from hunt alone does not fix
  `/gaze`. The rules recommend `-C` in four places (git.md:25, git.md:40,
  workflow.md:49, workflow.md:60), refused once entered.
- A parallel session ran the same experiment earlier (PR #1395, log entry
  16:00Z above, positive control an `isolation: worktree` subagent); its
  findings agree.
- A `cd` into the worktree moves the session's Bash cwd, but a session
  restart resets it to the launch directory, and so does a `cd` outside the
  project tree (the runtime resets the shell to the launch directory, not
  to the worktree): absolute paths remain the rule.
- The #1395 session's pid (3742738) was gone after that session ended:
  a process that hosted one session dies with it.
- Author's context in that session's launch prompt, not measured here: in
  a JETP session on 2026-10-14 the guard refused about 8 read-only
  commands (ssh reads of another host, `git -C <other repo> diff` loops, a
  sed loop over ticket files, a tmux launch whose prompt mentioned git)
  and caught nothing it should have.

## Decision (author, 2026-10-14)
Drop the guard: remove the `EnterWorktree` opt-in, no replacement guard.
Sessions create and lock their worktree with plain git and `cd` into it,
the convention Codex and Pi already follow. Accepted residual risk:
`restore`/`checkout` aimed at another tree lose their only guard (2 clear
catches in two months, census of 1074).

## Exit criteria
- [x] Experiment run and recorded (positive control: one refusal reproduced)
- [x] Author decision recorded
- [x] If removal decided: opt-in removed; `/hunt`, `/raid`, `/gaze` use plain git (from 1074)
- [ ] A raid's `/gaze` can create its review worktree, demonstrated once (from 1074)
- [x] `make check` passes (from 1074)
